<!-- =========================== 3. VERDICT =========================== -->
<section class="tab" id="tab-verdict">
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="hero">
    <span class="pill ok">Verdict: feasible</span>
    <h2>Yes — and code suits a knowledge graph better than documents do.</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="margin:0">The relationships you care about are already written down in code and config: calls, imports, table reads and writes, job → notebook, workflow → deployment. A parser extracts them exactly. In document GraphRAG an LLM has to guess them.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card"><div class="num">1</div><h4>Code already is a graph</h4><p>Functions call functions, files import files, jobs run notebooks, notebooks write tables, workflows deploy commits. Parsers read these edges, so there is no LLM guessing and almost no extraction cost.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="num">2</div><h4>Your best questions are multi-hop</h4><p>"What breaks if I change this?", "Who writes this table?", "Is my fix in prod yet?" Each answer is a path through 3–6 nodes, often across repos. Vector-only RAG can't follow paths. A graph can.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="num">3</div><h4>You already know the stack</h4><p>Neo4j, Cypher, OpenAI embeddings and a vector index are the same pieces as in your GVD course. What's new is a set of parsers, the GitHub API, and a sync job.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="callout key"><strong>The design decision that matters most</strong>Don't run document-style GraphRAG (an LLM extracting entities from text chunks) over code. Parse code deterministically, and use the LLM only for summaries, business concepts and writing the final answer.</div>

  <h3>Document GraphRAG vs code GraphRAG</h3>
  <div class="compare">
    <div class="ch"></div><div class="ch">Document GraphRAG</div><div class="ch good">Code GraphRAG (this design)</div>
    <div class="rh">Entities come from</div><div>LLM extraction over text chunks</div><div class="g">AST, SQL and YAML parsers</div>
    <div class="rh">Relationships</div><div>Guessed by the LLM ("related to")</div><div class="g">Exact: <code>CALLS</code>, <code>IMPORTS</code>, <code>READS</code>, <code>WRITES</code>, <code>RUNS</code>, <code>DEPLOYED_COMMIT</code>…</div>
    <div class="rh">Chunk unit</div><div>Token windows (for example 500 tokens)</div><div class="g">Function, class, notebook cell, doc section</div>
    <div class="rh">Build cost</div><div>Every chunk goes through an LLM</div><div class="g">Parsing is free; the LLM only writes summaries</div>
    <div class="rh">Precision</div><div>Medium; needs review</div><div class="g">High for structure, medium for meaning</div>
    <div class="rh">Freshness</div><div>Re-extract changed documents</div><div class="g">Re-parse only the files a commit changed</div>
    <div class="rh">Global questions</div><div>Community summaries</div><div class="g">Subsystem (community) summaries — same idea</div>
  </div>

  <h3>What the chatbot will be good at</h3>
  <div class="fit">
    <div class="fitcol strong"><h4>Strong <span>structure and status</span></h4><ul>
      <li>Where is X implemented, who calls it, what breaks if I change it?</li>
      <li>Which pipelines read or write a table; column lineage</li>
      <li>Which repos use package or version X; cross-repo dependencies</li>
      <li>CI status, failing builds, what's in prod vs. main</li>
      <li>Who owns or knows this code</li></ul></div>
    <div class="fitcol good"><h4>Good <span>meaning + graph context</span></h4><ul>
      <li>How do we usually do X (auth, logging, retries, secrets)?</li>
      <li>Explain this repo or module; onboarding tours</li>
      <li>Find similar code across repos</li>
      <li>Architecture overviews from subsystem summaries</li></ul></div>
    <div class="fitcol weak"><h4>Weak <span>needs other sources</span></h4><ul>
      <li>Why was it designed this way? → PRs, Jira, ADRs</li>
      <li>What did the job do at runtime, what's in the data? → Databricks run logs, Unity Catalog</li>
      <li>Table names built at runtime (f-strings, control tables) → partial</li>
      <li>Anything changed after the last sync → live MCP lookup</li></ul></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Where the GitHub MCP fits</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h4>At question time → GitHub MCP</h4><p>Use it for small, live lookups the chatbot makes while answering: the log of a failing job, the checks on an open PR, a file at HEAD. It is not built for bulk ingestion: it fetches one file per call, and a personal token gets 5,000 REST requests per hour.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>At ingestion time → git + REST/GraphQL</h4><p>A scheduled job clones each repo (one operation per repo, no per-file API calls) and pulls CI/CD metadata from the REST API: workflows, runs, environments, deployments, teams. Webhooks (<code>push</code>, <code>workflow_run</code>, <code>deployment_status</code>) keep it fresh.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="callout why"><strong>Configured: GitHub's official MCP server, read-only</strong>The remote endpoint <code>https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/</code> with the headers <code>X-MCP-Readonly: true</code> and <code>X-MCP-Toolsets: context,repos,git,actions,pull_requests,issues,orgs,users</code>. That exposes 32 read-only tools, for example <code>get_repository_tree</code>, <code>get_file_contents</code>, <code>search_code</code>, <code>list_commits</code> and <code>pull_request_read</code>, plus <code>actions_list</code>, <code>actions_get</code> and <code>get_job_logs</code> for CI status. It has no deployment or environment tools, so deployment status comes from the REST API in the ingestion job. It replaced the deprecated <code>@modelcontextprotocol/server-github</code>, which had write tools and no read-only mode.</div>
  <p class="small muted">Prior art for this pattern: Sourcegraph's code graph (SCIP indexes), Aider's repository map (tree-sitter + PageRank), and research systems such as CodexGraph (an LLM agent querying a Neo4j code graph with Cypher) and RepoGraph.</p>
</div>
</section>

