<!-- =========================== 4. QUERY =========================== -->
<section class="tab" id="tab-query">
<div class="wrap">
  <h2 class="sec">How the chatbot answers</h2>
  <p class="lead">An LLM agent with a few graph tools. It searches to find entry points, walks the graph to gather context, and answers with permalinks.</p>
  <div class="callout why"><strong>Built: <code>agent.py</code> + <code>tools.py</code>, with <code>chat_app.py</code> (Streamlit) and <code>chat_cli.py</code></strong>
    It uses plain OpenAI tool calling through your LiteLLM gateway: <code>gpt-5.3-chat</code> answers, with <code>gpt-5.2</code> as the fallback. Graph tools run as the read-only Neo4j user <code>codekg_reader</code>, and live data comes from the official GitHub MCP in read-only mode.
    After each answer, every link and backticked name is checked against the tool outputs. Anything unverified gets one repair turn and is then removed. A footer states the indexed commit or the sync time.
    <b>Measured</b> on 36 golden questions whose expected facts are taken from the graph, across impact, API, CI, deploy, people, overview, models, services, tables, how-to, counts, no-answer and live questions. The final run passed 36/36 at 7.4 s and 1.2 tool calls per answer. All 73 code links were pinned to a commit, and all 55 checked permalinks resolve to the file at that commit (<code>eval_chatbot.py</code>). Runs are traced to <code>data/traces/</code>, and to Langfuse through the gateway.</div>
  <div class="flow">
    <div class="stage"><span class="k">1</span><b>Question</b>"What breaks if I change read_config()?"</div><div class="arrow">›</div>
    <div class="stage"><span class="k">2</span><b>Route</b>Classify: lookup · impact · lineage · status · how-to · overview</div><div class="arrow">›</div>
    <div class="stage"><span class="k">3</span><b>Retrieve</b>Call 1–3 tools: search, expand, template, text-to-Cypher, MCP</div><div class="arrow">›</div>
    <div class="stage"><span class="k">4</span><b>Assemble</b>Code snippets + paths + facts from relationships + the "as of" commit</div><div class="arrow">›</div>
    <div class="stage"><span class="k">5</span><b>Answer</b>Cites GitHub permalinks; says "probably" for low-confidence edges</div>
  </div>

  <h3>The tools the agent gets</h3>
  <table class="tools">
    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>search_code(text)</code></td><td>Full-text on names plus vector search on summaries, returning entry nodes</td><td>"How do we…" and fuzzy questions</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>expand(ids)</code></td><td>Callers, callees, file, tests, tables and docs around the entry nodes</td><td>Context for nearly every answer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cypher templates</td><td><code>impact_of</code>, <code>api_map</code> (front end ↔ back end), <code>writers_of</code>, <code>lineage_of</code>, <code>owners_of</code>, <code>ci_health</code>, <code>whats_in_prod</code>, <code>app_overview</code></td><td>The frequent questions: fast and reliable</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>text_to_cypher(question)</code></td><td>The LLM writes Cypher from the schema plus examples, run by a read-only user</td><td>The long tail of structural questions</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>github_live(…)</code></td><td>The official GitHub MCP in read-only mode: <code>actions_list</code> / <code>actions_get</code> for run status, <code>get_job_logs</code> for failures, <code>pull_request_read</code> for PR checks, <code>get_file_contents</code> for a file at HEAD</td><td>When freshness matters</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Hybrid retrieval: names first, meaning second, then expand</h3>
  <script type="text/plain" class="code" data-lang="cypher" data-title="search_code">
// identifiers (full-text) + meaning (vector); merge the two lists in Python
CALL db.index.fulltext.queryNodes('code_names', $keywords) YIELD node, score
RETURN node.id AS id, score, 'name' AS hit LIMIT 5
UNION
CALL db.index.vector.queryNodes('code_embeddings', 8, $question_embedding) YIELD node, score
RETURN node.id AS id, score, 'meaning' AS hit
</script>
  <script type="text/plain" class="code" data-lang="cypher" data-title="expand">
MATCH (fn:Function) WHERE fn.id IN $ids
OPTIONAL MATCH (fn)-[:CALLS]->(callee:Function)
OPTIONAL MATCH (caller:Function)-[:CALLS]->(fn)
OPTIONAL MATCH (fn)-[rw:READS|WRITES]->(t:Table)
OPTIONAL MATCH (d:DocChunk)-[:MENTIONS]->(fn)
RETURN fn.id, fn.url, fn.summary, fn.code,
       collect(DISTINCT callee.qualified_name)[..5] AS calls,
       collect(DISTINCT caller.qualified_name)[..5] AS called_by,
       collect(DISTINCT type(rw) + ' ' + t.id) AS tables,
       collect(DISTINCT d.url)[..3] AS docs
</script>

  <h3>Guardrails</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h4>Answer rules</h4><ul class="small" style="margin:4px 0;padding-left:18px">
      <li>Cite permalinks pinned to the commit: <code>…/blob/&lt;sha&gt;/path#L52-L97</code>.</li>
      <li>Say "as of &lt;indexed_at&gt;" for code answers and "as of &lt;time&gt;" for status answers.</li>
      <li>When an edge has <code>confidence</code> below 0.7, say "probably", and offer the text-search fallback.</li>
      <li>Never invent table, function or repo names. If the graph has no answer, say so.</li></ul></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Text-to-Cypher safety</h4><ul class="small" style="margin:4px 0;padding-left:18px">
      <li>A read-only Neo4j user, a query timeout, and an enforced <code>LIMIT</code>.</li>
      <li>The schema (this page's graph) plus 10–20 example question → Cypher pairs in the prompt.</li>
      <li>Run <code>EXPLAIN</code> first; on an error, retry once with the error message.</li>
      <li>Prefer templates. Text-to-Cypher is the fallback, not the default.</li></ul></div>
  </div>

  <h3>How to build the agent</h3>
  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card"><h4>neo4j-graphrag</h4><p class="small">Neo4j's official Python package: <code>VectorCypherRetriever</code>, <code>HybridCypherRetriever</code> and <code>Text2CypherRetriever</code> cover search, expand and text-to-Cypher.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>LangChain / LangGraph</h4><p class="small"><code>langchain-neo4j</code> plus a LangGraph agent, if you want routing, memory and tool-calling graphs out of the box.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Plain OpenAI tool calling</h4><p class="small">The closest to your course code: define 5 tools that call <code>run()</code>, and let the model pick. Easiest to debug.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="callout why"><strong>Measure it</strong>Collect 30–50 real questions from the team, with expected answers. Score correctness and citation accuracy every time you change the schema or prompts, and trace runs (for example with Langfuse) to see which tool calls failed.</div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- =========================== 5. LIMITATIONS =========================== -->
<section class="tab" id="tab-limits">
<div class="wrap">
  <h2 class="sec">Limitations, and what to do about them</h2>
  <p class="lead">None of these is a blocker, but each one changes how much you can trust certain answers.</p>
  <div class="grid2" id="limGrid"></div>
  <h3>Rough sizing (illustrative)</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <p><b>Graph size</b> ≈ repos × files × (1 + symbols per file). For example, 40 repos × 250 files × 8 symbols ≈ <b>90,000 nodes</b> and a few hundred thousand relationships. That's small for Neo4j.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:8px"><b>Embeddings</b> ≈ 90,000 × ~400 tokens ≈ 36 M tokens: typically well under $10 with a small embedding model. <b>Summaries</b> push a similar number of tokens through a small chat model, so they are the main cost, and they are incremental after the first run. Check current pricing; these numbers are illustrative, not measured on your org.</p>
  </div>
</div>
