<section class="tab" id="tab-roadmap">
<div class="wrap">
  <h2 class="sec">Roadmap</h2>
  <p class="lead">Ship a useful MVP on a few repos first, then add layers. Each phase matches the phase numbers on the graph.</p>
  <div class="callout why"><strong>Status</strong>Phases 1 and 2 are built for all 80 repos in Neo4j <code>codekg</code>: code, dependencies, CI/CD runs, deployments, people, teams and the GenAI app layer.
    Also built: summaries and embeddings through the company LiteLLM gateway, 69 Subsystem communities, the chatbot with text-to-Cypher, the code clock, and a 36-question evaluation.
    Not yet built: column lineage, Concepts, access-control filtering per user, an evaluation dashboard, and webhooks (they need a server that GitHub can reach).</div>
  <div class="tline">
    <div class="tphase"><h4>Phase 0 · Access &amp; inventory</h4><div class="when">1–2 days</div><ul>
      <li>A read-only token that satisfies the org's access policies (checklist below). Inventory the repos: languages, sizes, workflows, environments.</li>
      <li>Pick 2–3 pilot repos, ideally a shared library plus two consumers, so cross-repo links show up.</li>
      <li>Write 30 golden questions the team really asks, with expected answers.</li></ul></div>
    <div class="tphase"><h4>Phase 1 · MVP code graph + chatbot</h4><div class="when">about 1–2 weeks</div><ul>
      <li>Repository, File, Class, Function, Package, DocChunk with CONTAINS, DEFINES, HAS_METHOD, CALLS, IMPORTS, DEPENDS_ON, MENTIONS.</li>
      <li>Function summaries + embeddings, a vector index and a full-text index.</li>
      <li>A chatbot with <code>search_code</code> + <code>expand</code>, answering with permalinks.</li></ul>
      <div class="exit"><b>Suggested exit check:</b> most golden "where / how" questions are answered correctly, with working citations.</div></div>
    <div class="tphase"><h4>Phase 2 · Data lineage, CI/CD, ownership</h4><div class="when">about 2–3 weeks</div><ul>
      <li>Table, Pipeline, Task with READS, WRITES, DERIVED_FROM, RUNS, DEPLOYED_TO.</li>
      <li>Workflow, WorkflowRun, Environment, Deployment, Commit: CI status and "what's in prod".</li>
      <li>Person, Team, CODEOWNERS; PUBLISHED_FROM for cross-repo calls; Cypher templates; both sync clocks.</li></ul>
      <div class="exit"><b>Suggested exit check:</b> impact, table-writer, CI-health and what's-in-prod questions are answered from the graph.</div></div>
    <div class="tphase"><h4>Phase 3 · Depth &amp; governance</h4><div class="when">ongoing</div><ul>
      <li>Column lineage, ConfigKey, Action, PullRequest, Ticket (Jira), Subsystem communities, Concept glossary.</li>
      <li>Text-to-Cypher for the long tail, access-control filtering, an evaluation dashboard.</li></ul></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Access checklist</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card"><h4>Token for the pilot</h4><ul class="small" style="margin:4px 0;padding-left:18px">
      <li>A <b>fine-grained</b> token whose resource owner is <code>Cepheid-Data-Platform</code> (not your user account), with repository access "All repositories".</li>
      <li>Read-only repository permissions: Contents, Metadata, Actions, Deployments, Environments, Pull requests, Issues, Commit statuses. Organization permission: Members (read), for teams.</li>
      <li><b>Org-owner approval</b>, if the org requires it for fine-grained tokens. (A classic token would instead need SAML SSO authorization.)</li>
      <li><b>Satisfy the conditional access policy.</b> The enterprise checks each token request against its identity provider's rules. If the network isn't allowed, GitHub answers "does not satisfy the organization's or enterprise's conditional access policies". Ask the GitHub / Entra admins which network is allowed for token access.</li></ul></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>For production</h4><ul class="small" style="margin:4px 0;padding-left:18px">
      <li>A <b>GitHub App</b> installed on the org instead of a personal token: no personal credentials, higher rate limits, webhooks included. Ask the admins whether this is the approved route for automation under the conditional access policy.</li>
      <li>Keep the chatbot's GitHub MCP <b>read-only</b>, and give the agent no write tools.</li>
      <li>Store tokens in a secret store or a password-protected input, not in plain-text config files.</li></ul></div>
  </div>
